in that quantum state

01:14:56.760 --> 01:15:01.720
can only have 0 and
1, discrete, OK?

01:15:01.720 --> 01:15:06.100
So when I have here,
Boltzmann factor

01:15:06.100 --> 01:15:10.850
is a [? foreclosed ?] system
at constant temperature.

01:15:10.850 --> 01:15:13.700
And the [? one ?]
ideal is an open system

01:15:13.700 --> 01:15:17.660
at a constant temperature,
I have to modify this.

01:15:17.660 --> 01:15:20.150
So I'm going to
give you, again--

01:15:20.150 --> 01:15:22.530
just wait for the next lecture--

01:15:22.530 --> 01:15:23.555
say, the Gibbs factor.

01:15:29.830 --> 01:15:32.140
And Gibbs factor
is for open system.

01:15:32.140 --> 01:15:37.960
And the probability of
a system, open system,

01:15:37.960 --> 01:15:41.840
has a certain energy and
certain number of particle

01:15:41.840 --> 01:15:52.040
is at temperature T is A, again,
normalization minus the energy

01:15:52.040 --> 01:15:56.560
of the system minus
the number of particle

01:15:56.560 --> 01:15:59.595
times this constant
mu and divide by kBT.

01:16:02.200 --> 01:16:06.790
So now, I add this mu, which
is the chemical potential.

01:16:06.790 --> 01:16:11.620
That's to say how much energy
you need to take out or place

01:16:11.620 --> 01:16:14.130
a particle into the system.

01:16:14.130 --> 01:16:17.430
That's the chemical
potential, OK?

01:16:17.430 --> 01:16:19.980
So mu is the chemical potential.

01:16:33.100 --> 01:16:37.510
And that's the same chemical
potential is, essentially,

01:16:37.510 --> 01:16:38.560
the Gibbs free energy.

01:16:41.690 --> 01:16:45.320
And it doesn't include
the entropy part,

01:16:45.320 --> 01:16:47.480
and Gibbs free energy
difference gives

01:16:47.480 --> 01:16:52.620
you how much work you can
get out of the system, OK?

01:16:52.620 --> 01:17:00.850
So with that, I'm considering
one isolated electron quantum

01:17:00.850 --> 01:17:01.350
state.

01:17:04.350 --> 01:17:06.180
This is the easiest.

01:17:06.180 --> 01:17:12.150
So I have one quantum
state of electron, right?

01:17:12.150 --> 01:17:16.140
This is the solution you
had before in quantum--

01:17:16.140 --> 01:17:21.420
either for solid or for a
molecule or for atom, right?

01:17:21.420 --> 01:17:24.370
You have the solution
of quantum state.

01:17:24.370 --> 01:17:26.450
This is what we've been doing.

01:17:26.450 --> 01:17:30.290
The question is whether there
is the electron in the quantum

01:17:30.290 --> 01:17:31.490
state or not.

01:17:31.490 --> 01:17:36.380
So what's the probability?
is it 0 or [? 1, ?] right?

01:17:36.380 --> 01:17:39.270
Well, say, these
are the two states.

01:17:39.270 --> 01:17:44.850
When n equals 0 or
1, when n equals 0,

01:17:44.850 --> 01:17:47.160
even though you have
the quantum state,

01:17:47.160 --> 01:17:49.620
that state doesn't have
real electron pair,

01:17:49.620 --> 01:17:51.730
doesn't have energy.

01:17:51.730 --> 01:17:56.750
So the energy when
this is 0, this is 0.

01:17:56.750 --> 01:17:59.420
And when you have one
particle that energy,

01:17:59.420 --> 01:18:03.230
that state has energy Ei, right?

01:18:03.230 --> 01:18:05.870
So Ei.

01:18:05.870 --> 01:18:10.350
Now, I use this,
two possibility.

01:18:10.350 --> 01:18:14.490
I do my normalization
factor for the Gibbs factor.

01:18:14.490 --> 01:18:20.840
So add up, it has to be
either 0 or [? 1, ?] right?

01:18:20.840 --> 01:18:23.590
They add [? up ?]
[? who has ?] to be what?

01:18:23.590 --> 01:18:30.880
So when it's 0, p
0 0 T, plus p here

01:18:30.880 --> 01:18:36.040
is Ei 1 T. That has to be 1.

01:18:36.040 --> 01:18:41.950
So the first, when 0 0,
that's just A. The second one,

01:18:41.950 --> 01:18:46.360
Ei, so I have minus Ei.

01:18:46.360 --> 01:18:52.870
Mu times n is just
mu kBT equals 1.

01:18:52.870 --> 01:18:58.990
So this gives me A
equals exponential

01:18:58.990 --> 01:19:06.400
minus Ei minus mu kBT plus 1.

01:19:06.400 --> 01:19:12.470
That's my normalization
factor, A, right?

01:19:12.470 --> 01:19:17.550
Now, my average
number of particle F,

01:19:17.550 --> 01:19:23.030
average number, which is the
average number of n, right?

01:19:23.030 --> 01:19:26.600
Once I have distribution
probability,

01:19:26.600 --> 01:19:30.860
I can find the average, any
quantity, average energy,

01:19:30.860 --> 01:19:34.010
average number of particle,
whatever average you want,

01:19:34.010 --> 01:19:34.910
right?

01:19:34.910 --> 01:19:45.260
So that should be 0 because
0 times this p 0 0 T plus 1.

01:19:45.260 --> 01:19:53.030
I have one particle
times p Ei 1 T. So

01:19:53.030 --> 01:19:56.420
that's my average
number of particle,

01:19:56.420 --> 01:20:01.040
right, because this is the
probability when Ei 1 fully

01:20:01.040 --> 01:20:02.480
empty.

01:20:02.480 --> 01:20:08.670
And you go-- you know
your A. You know your p--

01:20:08.670 --> 01:20:12.360
A time this, Ei mu n.

01:20:12.360 --> 01:20:18.060
So you can say this one and
this one is the same, right?

01:20:18.060 --> 01:20:20.040
In the numerator,
I have this one.

01:20:20.040 --> 01:20:21.540
In the denominator,
I have this one,

01:20:21.540 --> 01:20:26.890
but I have a plus
factor 1, right?

01:20:26.890 --> 01:20:30.850
So I derive this so I
can write a simpler--

01:20:30.850 --> 01:20:38.800
and you will see now what I
have is Ei minus mu kBT plus 1.

01:20:38.800 --> 01:20:57.200
And this is the famous
Fermi-Dirac distribution, OK?

01:20:57.200 --> 01:21:03.620
So in three steps, right, if you
take that energy conversation,

01:21:03.620 --> 01:21:08.550
probably normalization,
you got a Fermi-Dirac.

01:21:08.550 --> 01:21:13.510
And the next lecture
we'll go a few more steps,

01:21:13.510 --> 01:21:17.100
put it together, Bose-Einstein
distribution for phonons.

01:21:17.100 --> 01:21:19.400
This is for electrons.

